  "final_message": "---\n\n## \ud83d\udccb Final Summary\n\n**Winning strategy: EMPTY (keep default)**\n\n### What was found\n| block_threads | Run 1 vs_default | Run 2 vs_default | Run 3 vs_default | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 128 | 1.177 | \u2014 | \u2014 | \u274c Worse |\n| 256 | 1.067 | \u2014 | \u2014 | \u274c Worse |\n| 512 | 1.004 | \u2014 | \u2014 | \u274c Worse |\n| **1024** | **0.939** | **0.988** | **1.047** | \u26a0\ufe0f Noisy / inconclusive |\n\n### Why I stopped\n`block_threads=1024` showed a promising first measurement (6.1% speedup, `vs_default=0.939`), but across three consecutive runs the `vs_default` swung from **0.939 \u2192 0.988 \u2192 1.047**, with `meas_spread` consistently above 0.38\u201319.2. The kernel is operating at ~10 \u00b5s latency \u2014 at this scale, clock jitter and GPU scheduling noise dominate. **The DECISIVE MARGIN RULE requires vs_default \u2264 0.97 reproduced on at least two calls; this was never satisfied.**\n\n### Recommendation\nThe default compiler configuration is optimal for `softmax` on `[32, 4096]` at this hardware scale. The problem is too small (32 rows \u00d7 4096 cols = 131K elements) for any block-thread tuning to produce a robust, reproducible gain over the well-tuned default kernel. Ship the **empty strategy**.",
  "stop_reason": "stop"
